.SS Rendering
.I curses
combines the following data to render a character cell.
Any of them can include color information.
.bP
.I curses
character and attributes,
as from \fB\%waddch\fP(3X)
.bP
window attributes,
as from \fB\%wattrset\fP(3X)
.bP
window background character attributes,
as from \fB\%wbkgdset\fP(3X)
.PP
Per-character and window attributes are usually set through a function
parameter containing attributes and/or a color pair identifier.
Some functions,
such as
.BR \%wattr_set ","
use a separate color pair identifier.
.PP
The background character is a special case:
it includes a character code,
as if it were passed to
.BR \%waddch "."
.PP
The
.I curses
library does the actual work of combining these color pairs
in an internal function called from
.BR \%waddch "."
.bP
If the parameter passed to
.B \%waddch
is blank and it uses the special color pair 0,
.I curses
next checks the window attribute.
.RS
.bP
If the window attribute does not use color pair 0,
.I curses
uses the color pair from the window attribute.
.bP
Otherwise,
.I curses
uses the background character.
.RE
.bP
If the parameter passed to
.B \%waddch
is not blank,
or it does not use the special color pair 0,
.I curses
prefers the color pair from the parameter,
if it is nonzero.
Otherwise,
it tries the window attribute next,
and finally the background character.
.PP
Some
.I curses
functions such as \fB\%wprintw\fP(3X) call
.BR \%waddch "."
Those do not combine its parameter with a color pair.
Consequently those calls use only the window attribute
or the background character.
.\"
